       The  wwpprriinnttff()  family of functions is the wide‐character equivalent of
       the pprriinnttff(3) family of functions.  It  performs  formatted  output  of
       wide characters.

       The wwpprriinnttff() and vvwwpprriinnttff() functions perform wide‐character output to
       _s_t_d_o_u_t.  _s_t_d_o_u_t must not be byte oriented; see ffwwiiddee(3) for more infor‐
       mation.

       The  ffwwpprriinnttff() and vvffwwpprriinnttff() functions perform wide‐character output
       to _s_t_r_e_a_m.  _s_t_r_e_a_m must not be byte oriented; see ffwwiiddee(3) for more in‐
       formation.

       The sswwpprriinnttff() and vvsswwpprriinnttff() functions perform wide‐character  output
       to  an array of wide characters.  The programmer must ensure that there
       is room for at least _n wide characters at _w_c_s.

       These functions are like the  pprriinnttff(3),  vvpprriinnttff(3),  ffpprriinnttff(3),  vvff‐‐
       pprriinnttff(3),  sspprriinnttff(3),  vvsspprriinnttff(3) functions except for the following
       differences:

       ••      The _f_o_r_m_a_t string is a wide‐character string.

       ••      The output consists of wide characters, not bytes.

       ••      sswwpprriinnttff() and vvsswwpprriinnttff() take a  _n  argument,  sspprriinnttff(3)  and
              vvsspprriinnttff(3)  do not.  (ssnnpprriinnttff(3) and vvssnnpprriinnttff(3) take a _n ar‐
              gument, but these functions do not return -1 upon  buffer  over‐
